<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Novocherkassk</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Novocherkassk is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostov">Rostov</a> oblast, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, founded in 1805 by Ataman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matvei-platov">Matvei Platov</a> as the new capital of the Don <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> Host. It was a centre of the anti-Bolshevik Don Cossacks in the Russian Civil War, and in 1962 a workers' protest there was put down with gunfire by Soviet troops, the Novocherkassk massacre. Its large Ascension Cathedral was completed in 1905.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/novocherkassk.jpg" alt="Novocherkassk" width="320" height="251" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Denis Putilin at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Novoch_Triumph_arc.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Novocherkassk, whose name means New Cherkassk, stands near the confluence of the Tuzlov and Aksay rivers in Rostov Oblast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. The Aksay is a distributary of the Don. The city is best known as the cultural capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> and the official capital of the Don Cossacks. It lies on a hill known as the Wolf's Lair, north of the older centre of Cherkassk.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matvei-platov">Matvei Platov</a>, Ataman of the Don Cossacks, founded the city in 1805 as the administrative centre of the Don Host Oblast. Cherkassk was judged unsuitable: it lay low on the Don and was repeatedly flooded, and its wooden buildings made it prone to fires. Ten of the eleven representatives of its villages refused to move, but Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> granted permission by a decree of 23 August 1804. Platov and the engineer François Sainte de Wollant planned a city with wide avenues and squares, which De Wollant called a little <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>.</p><p>Building was slow, since most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> were reluctant to leave their homes and the new capital was far from the Don. In 1837 Tsar Nicholas I inspected it and another site and ordered the capital kept where it was. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a> the city was the centre of the Don Army counter-revolution under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexey-kaledin">Alexey Kaledin</a>, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> drove the White-aligned Don Army out on 7 January 1920. German forces occupied it from 24 July 1942 to 13 February 1943. On 1 and 2 June 1962 food riots and a strike at the locomotive factory led to the Novocherkassk massacre, in which Soviet troops killed 26 protesters and wounded 87.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In 1991 a meeting of Don Cossack organisations confirmed Novocherkassk as the capital of the Don Cossacks, and in 1993 it was proclaimed the world capital of the Cossacks. It has a large neo-Byzantine cathedral, the palace of the ataman and monuments to Platov and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yermak-timofeyevich">Yermak Timofeyevich</a>. A monument opened in 2005 is dedicated to the reconciliation of White and Red Cossacks.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Novocherkassk” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Over the 18th century the Host lost its self-government and became a privileged military estate of the Russian Empire, with its capital moved to <b>Novocherkassk</b> in 1805.</blockquote><a href="/state/don-cossack-host">Don Cossack Host</a></li><li><blockquote>In May 1918 a Cossack assembly in <b>Novocherkassk</b> elected General Pyotr Krasnov ataman and proclaimed the independent Almighty Don Host, heir to the Don Cossack Host.</blockquote><a href="/state/don-republic">Don Republic</a></li></ul></section>
